<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    AppServices (C125) — the lightweight production replica's Container 7.

    `specs/lightweight-production-replica.md` §"Container 7 — app-services" co-locates the domain
    services and the YARP gateway in ONE .NET process to stay inside the 24 GB VPS budget. This
    project is that process and contains no domain logic: every service is built by its own
    `XApplication.Build`, exactly as in production, and this host only decides where each listens,
    what order they start in, and which loopback address the gateway is told to route to.

    TWENTY-TWO, NOT THE SPEC'S TWENTY-ONE. The spec's table predates two services that now exist and
    have no other container to live in: `payout-svc` (C061) and `fleet-billing-svc` (C060). The dev
    compose already says "the 22 domain services", so the count here follows the code rather than the
    table. `ocr-svc` is in the spec's list and is here, which is why this container needs its own
    Dockerfile — see infra/docker/Dockerfile.appservices for the native Tesseract/OpenCV story.

    THREE OF THE GATEWAY'S CLUSTERS ARE NOT IN THIS CONTAINER and must not be overridden here:
    `fanout-svc` is Container 8, `fleet-health-svc` is inside Container 6, and `voip-svc` is the
    optional Container 11. Their addresses come from the compose environment. Overriding them here
    would point the gateway at a port in this process that nothing is listening on.

    WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL, AND WHY SO LATE. `infra/docker-compose.dev.yml` has referenced
    `backend/src/AppServices/Dockerfile` since C009 and nothing created it. C118 went PARTIAL because
    the dev stack could not be brought up without it and deferred it to "C124/C125"; C124 then built
    34 per-service images for the D7' §5 Kubernetes layout, where each of these 22 IS its own
    Deployment. The co-located form the replica needs had never existed.

    THE REPLICA'S LAYOUT IS NOT PRODUCTION'S, AND THAT IS THE SPEC'S CHOICE. The spec's own note is
    the reason this host can be so thin: "Each service is a separate class library with its own DI
    registration, so extracting to individual containers for production is a configuration change,
    not a code change." In this repository each is a separate ASP.NET application with its own
    `Build`, which makes that even more true than the note claims.

    Verify: `infra/replica/deploy.sh` with the dry-run flag. (Written out rather than pasted: an XML
    comment cannot carry a double hyphen.)
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>MageRide.AppServices</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>MageRide.AppServices</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <UserSecretsId>mageride-appservices-host</UserSecretsId>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The edge. Started last, so every cluster it fronts is already listening. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\ApiGateway\ApiGateway.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The 22 domain services of Container 7, in the spec's own order where it gives one. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Iam.Api\Iam.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Registry.Api\Registry.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Provisioning.Api\Provisioning.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Query.Api\Query.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\TripState.Api\TripState.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ride.Api\Ride.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Dispatch.Api\Dispatch.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Fare.Api\Fare.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Subscription.Api\Subscription.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Wallet.Api\Wallet.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Notification.Api\Notification.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Safety.Api\Safety.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Reputation.Api\Reputation.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Content.Api\Content.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Support.Api\Support.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Fleet.Api\Fleet.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\AdminBff\AdminBff.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ocr.Api\Ocr.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Transit.Api\Transit.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PublicBff\PublicBff.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Payout.Api\Payout.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FleetBilling\FleetBilling.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The co-location mechanics are HotPath's; sharing them is what keeps the two containers' -->
    <!-- start-up, shutdown and port-assignment behaviour identical. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\HotPath\HotPath.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Five of the referenced projects ship an appsettings.json (api-gateway, iam-svc, registry-svc,
    provisioning-svc, trip-state-svc); the other seventeen ship none and configure themselves from
    the environment. Publishing all of them into one directory is NETSDK1152 - "multiple publish
    output files with the same relative path" - and suppressing that error would be worse than the
    error: one of the five would win arbitrarily and the other four would silently read its values.

    Co-location makes that unavoidable rather than accidental. Every service in this container shares
    one content root, so `appsettings.json` can only ever hold ONE service's settings whatever the
    build does. The honest resolution is that this container is configured from the environment - which
    is exactly how it is deployed: infra/replica/ and infra/docker-compose.dev.yml both load
    env/.env.common.example and env/.env.app.example into it, and every service's option validator
    fails loudly at start-up if something it needs is absent.

    So the referenced copies are dropped and this project's own appsettings.json is the only one. It
    carries the shared defaults; anything service-specific belongs in the environment. gateway-routes.json
    is NOT an appsettings file and is deliberately untouched - the gateway reads it by name.
  -->
  <Target Name="DropCoLocatedAppSettings" AfterTargets="ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_ForeignAppSettings
        Include="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)"
        Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(RelativePath)').StartsWith('appsettings'))
                   and !$([System.String]::Copy('%(Identity)').Contains('$(MSBuildProjectName)'))" />
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Remove="@(_ForeignAppSettings)" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Message Importance="high"
             Text="AppServices: dropped @(_ForeignAppSettings->Count()) appsettings file(s) from referenced projects; this container is configured from the environment." />
  </Target>

</Project>
